  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Integrated AIDS Project, Thika</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Integrated AIDS Programme (IAP) Thika continues to provide services aimed to prevent the spread of HIV and to provide quality care in order to improve the quality of life for people infected with and affected by HIV and AIDS. The programme has three pronged interventions of Home Based Care (HBC), Education for Life (EFL) and Orphans and Vulnerable Children (OVC). Specific programme activities include medical care, VCT services, on going counselling, psychosocial support, pastoral care, outreach clinics, AIDS and health education, material support and home visits. This project (KEN563) focuses provision of static and mobile VCT services, community mobilisation, HIV and AIDS and health education and home care including support to OVCs. These interventions are targeted to rural communities to ensure that these communities easily access HIV and AIDS and health services. 
This project complements the all programme components. It plays a critical role as the entry point to the care programme. HBC teams intensify home visits and provide back up support to Community Health Workers (CHWs) and family level caregivers. Comprehensive Care Centre (CCC) provides specialised care and treatment to clients. Referrals to Gatundu and/or Thika hospitals for further examinations and treatment are made as appropriate. EFL has refocused Behaviour Change Process (BCP) sessions within schools to link with VCT services to ensure that the youth have adequate information enable them make informed choices. 
OVC teams continue to build capacity at village level where guardians&#x2019; self help groups have started to provide basic care and support to OVCs. New initiatives on improving domestic incomes for both OVCs and guardians are providing useful lessons for future economic empowerment and programme sustainability. VCT services through this project have intensified to provide close to home services to the local communities making the entire programme a reality. VCT sites are fully operational. A combination of mobile VCTs with the door to door VCT approach is desirable for wider reach and has enabled couples to access HIV and AIDS services than before.</narrative>
  </description>
